From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: do not touch a gated core from an idle poll

The completion poll queues a work item from the hrtimer and retires the
job from rocket_job_handle_irq(), which opens with OPERATION_ENABLE = 0
and INTERRUPT_CLEAR before it looks at what is in flight.  For an
interrupt those two writes are the acknowledgement and have to happen
whatever the state; for the poll they are neither.  It sampled a status
register, so there is no condition to end, and if the submit it was
queued for has already been retired -- by the DMA-error interrupt thread,
or by a reset -- there is no block left to stop either.

That retire also ran pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(), so by the time the
work is scheduled the core may be runtime-suspended: the writes then
reach an NPU whose clocks are gated, which on this SoC is not a lost
write but a hang.

poll_seq does not cover this.  It advances only in
rocket_job_hw_submit(), so it catches a retire followed by another submit
-- the case it was added for -- and not a retire with nothing behind it,
where the work still looks current because nothing has been submitted
since.  The state that distinguishes them is in_flight_job.

Tell rocket_job_handle_irq() which path it is on and let the poll return
early when the core is idle.  The interrupt path is unchanged: its
condition is level-triggered, so it clears whatever is in flight,
including nothing.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 37fa9caac..fb1540f6d 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -641,7 +641,7 @@ static struct dma_fence *rocket_job_run(struct drm_sched_job *sched_job)
 	return fence;
 }
 
-static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core);
+static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core, bool from_poll);
 
 static enum hrtimer_restart rocket_poll_timer_fn(struct hrtimer *timer)
 {
@@ -723,10 +723,10 @@ static void rocket_poll_work_fn(struct work_struct *work)
 	if (core->poll_work_seq != core->poll_seq)
 		return;
 
-	rocket_job_handle_irq(core);
+	rocket_job_handle_irq(core, true);
 }
 
-static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core)
+static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core, bool from_poll)
 {
 	u32 clear = 0x1ffff;
 
@@ -741,6 +741,24 @@ static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core)
 	pm_runtime_mark_last_busy(core->dev);
 
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
+		/*
+		 * The poll took no interrupt, so it has no hardware condition to
+		 * acknowledge: with the submit it was queued for already retired --
+		 * by the DMA-error thread or by a reset -- and nothing resubmitted
+		 * behind it, there is no block to stop and nothing to clear.  The
+		 * retire it missed also dropped the runtime-PM reference, so the
+		 * core can be suspended by now and these writes would reach a gated
+		 * NPU.  poll_seq does not cover this: it only advances in
+		 * rocket_job_hw_submit(), so a retire with no submit after it leaves
+		 * the work looking current.
+		 *
+		 * The interrupt path has the opposite obligation.  Its condition is
+		 * level-triggered and INTERRUPT_CLEAR is what ends it, so it clears
+		 * whatever is in flight, including nothing.
+		 */
+		if (from_poll && !core->in_flight_job)
+			return;
+
 		/*
 		 * Stopping the block belongs under the lock.  rocket_job_hw_submit()
 		 * writes OPERATION_ENABLE from inside it, so this zero landing after
@@ -870,7 +888,7 @@ static irqreturn_t rocket_job_irq_handler_thread(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct rocket_core *core = data;
 
-	rocket_job_handle_irq(core);
+	rocket_job_handle_irq(core, false);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
